course: dictionary
lesson: 6063
title: Mere: only, just, no more than
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word mere means only, just, or no more than. It makes something sound small or unimportant. Read this: «Some rooms are mere cupboards, others nearly as broad and imposing as Toad's dining-hall.» Mere tells you the first rooms are only cupboards: small, not grand.
explain: Use the swap test. Put just in place of mere: «Some rooms are just cupboards» still makes sense, so mere means just here. Imposing is another clue: it means big and grand, and the other rooms are that, so the cupboards are the opposite.
explain: Mere is also used to show that something is small in size or amount. «The merest touch» means the smallest possible touch, and «it costs a mere £2» means the price is small. Look at the words next to mere to see whether it is shrinking something.
explain: Be careful with one old use. In old or poetic writing, a mere is a noun meaning a lake or pond, as in some English place names. Check the sentence: if it is about a lake, it is the noun; if it is about small or only, it is the adjective.
fact: Mere means only, just, or no more than.
fact: «It costs a mere £2» means the price is small.
fact: «The merest touch» means the smallest possible touch.
fact: Merely is the adverb form, meaning only or just.
fact: In old or poetic writing, a mere is a lake or pond, as in some English place names.
exercise: «It costs a mere £2.» What does mere tell you about the price? | The price is paid in gold coins | The shop is closed | *The price is small | The price is very large
exercise: In «Some rooms are mere cupboards, others nearly as broad and imposing as Toad's dining-hall», which word means big and grand? | rooms | mere | cupboards | *imposing
exercise: What does «the merest touch» mean? | A hard push | A long hug | A loud noise | *The smallest possible touch
test: In «the mere thought of spiders makes me shiver», what does mere mean? | *Just the thought, nothing more | Happy | A pond full of spiders | A very long thought
test: In old or poetic writing, what is a mere? | A grand dining-hall | *A lake or pond | A kind of shiver | A small cupboard
exam: In «a mere boy beat the giant», what does mere show? | *The boy was small and only a boy, which makes his win surprising | The boy was a giant too | The boy was a lake | The boy was ancient
